9
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:11,520
One cent.

10
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:12,560
That is the trick.

11
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:13,280
Turns out,

12
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:14,960
your brain reads a price left to

13
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:15,360
right,

14
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,360
and mostly stops at the first digit.

15
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,560
Four dollars looks like a four.

16
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:21,520
Three ninety-nine looks like a three.

17
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:23,040
So your brain rounds it down,

18
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:24,960
and the cheaper-feeling number wins.

19
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,040
Studies find that prices just under a

20
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,280
round number tend to sell more than

21
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:30,720
one cent should explain.

22
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:32,320
But the lamp is still worth about

23
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:33,120
four dollars.

24
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,560
So read the left digit,

25
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:36,400
then ask what it is really worth.

26
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,600
The shopkeeper knows.

27
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,320
He still sells lamps at 3.99.

28
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:40,720
Now,

29
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:42,400
what does a price ending in zero

30
